# Secrets. docker compose reads .env from beside docker-compose.yml, so a real key
# WILL land here the moment one is configured - it must never be committable.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
credentials*.json

.claude/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
node_modules/
web/dist/
.panelbuilder_cache/
*.tgz

# data/maps (the genetic map) and tests/fixtures (recorded API responses, gzipped) are
# committed on purpose: the image builds and the suite runs with no network. Do not ignore.

# Host-specific: internal hostnames, LAN addresses, a tunnel id, uid pinning. Kept out of
# the public repo; `docker compose up -d --build` is the whole public story. The deploy
# tarball ships them because it copies the working tree, not the git index.
deploy/README-deploy.md
docker-compose.override.yml

# The audit's inputs are 500 MB of public GEO arrays; the record and the reports are committed,
# the arrays are re-downloadable from the accessions listed in audit/AUDIT.txt.
audit/raw/
audit/out/

# Validation scripts keyed to internal sample identifiers from unpublished experiments, and the
# preview scaffolding built on their output. The measurements they produced are quoted in the
# changelog and the documentation; the identifiers are not ours to publish. `tools/parity_check.py`
# and `tools/step5_public_validation.py` run on public GEO accessions and ARE committed.
#
# These files are not in the working tree either, and that is the actual control. Ignoring them
# only keeps them out of the git index: `Dockerfile` does `COPY web/ ./` and the deploy tarball
# copies the working tree, so a gitignored file under web/src still reaches an image layer. This
# block is the guard for anyone who regenerates them in place.
tools/step1_validate.py
tools/step2_bootstrap.py
tools/step2b_groups.py
tools/step3_depth.py
web/src/PreviewProducts.tsx
web/src/previewData50402.json
